WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.887
Collections are where many borrow checker
lessons become practical.

2
00:00:03.967 --> 00:00:05.976
Borrowing one element from a vector, map,

3
00:00:06.056 --> 00:00:09.787
or string can prevent mutation of the
collection while that borrow is active.

4
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:13.483
That protection matters because changing a
collection may move elements,

5
00:00:13.563 --> 00:00:17.294
resize storage, or invalidate assumptions
about what a reference points to.

6
00:00:17.374 --> 00:00:20.244
Iteration choices also communicate
ownership and access.

7
00:00:20.324 --> 00:00:22.218
Iterating by value can consume values.

8
00:00:22.298 --> 00:00:25.742
Iterating by shared reference reads values
without taking ownership.

9
00:00:25.822 --> 00:00:28.578
Iterating by mutable reference changes
values in place.

10
00:00:28.658 --> 00:00:32.676
A loop should choose the access pattern
that matches the work it actually performs.

11
00:00:32.756 --> 00:00:36.716
Borrow conflicts often happen when code
tries to read from a collection and modify

12
00:00:36.796 --> 00:00:37.772
it in the same phase.

13
00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:41.182
For example, a function might look up an
entry, keep a reference to it,

14
00:00:41.262 --> 00:00:44.533
and then try to insert or remove something
from the same collection.

15
00:00:44.613 --> 00:00:48.345
The compiler rejects these patterns when
the active reference would make the

16
00:00:48.425 --> 00:00:49.802
mutation unclear or unsafe.

17
00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:52.867
Defensive refactoring separates lookup
from modification.

18
00:00:52.947 --> 00:00:56.219
First collect the information needed to
decide what should happen.

19
00:00:56.299 --> 00:00:57.676
Then let the read borrow end.

20
00:00:57.756 --> 00:00:59.249
Finally perform the mutation.

21
00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:01.740
In other cases, use APIs that safely split
access,

22
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.427
borrow only the necessary field,

23
00:01:03.507 --> 00:01:06.607
or return an owned value that can outlive
the collection borrow.

24
00:01:06.687 --> 00:01:08.983
Avoid reflexive cloning as the first
response.

25
00:01:09.063 --> 00:01:12.220
Cloning can be right when independent
ownership is truly needed,

26
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.137
but it can also hide unclear data flow.

27
00:01:14.217 --> 00:01:15.996
Narrow borrow scopes, split phases,

28
00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:20.209
and clearer function boundaries often
solve the problem while preserving the design

29
00:01:20.289 --> 00:01:20.691
intent.

30
00:01:20.771 --> 00:01:24.100
When cloning is chosen, it should be
intentional and easy to explain.
